# Local development configuration — see docs/23_ENVIRONMENT_CONFIGURATION.md.
# Copy this file to .env (never commit the real .env — see .gitignore) and fill in real values.
#
# Only used for development builds (AppEnvironment.development, the default). Staging/production
# builds always get their backend URL from --dart-define=API_BASE_URL=... instead — see
# docs/23_ENVIRONMENT_CONFIGURATION.md.
#
# Default below is USB development (recommended): a physical Android device connected over USB,
# with `adb reverse tcp:4000 tcp:4000` forwarding the device's own 127.0.0.1:4000 to this machine's
# 127.0.0.1:4000. Immune to Wi-Fi changes/LAN IP churn — never needs editing again once set. Run
# `frontend/scripts/run_usb.sh` instead of `flutter run` to set up the port forward automatically.
#
# Platform-specific notes:
#   - Physical device via USB (default, see above): http://127.0.0.1:4000
#   - Physical device via Wi-Fi:  http://<YOUR_MACHINE_LAN_IP>:<port>  — MUST be on the same Wi-Fi
#                        network as your development machine; update this every time that IP
#                        changes (different Wi-Fi, mobile hotspot, router DHCP renewal), then
#                        hot-restart. Find your machine's current LAN IP:
#                          macOS:   ipconfig getifaddr en0   (Wi-Fi, or `en1` depending on your Mac)
#                          Linux:   hostname -I
#                          Windows: ipconfig   (look for "IPv4 Address" under your active adapter)
#   - Android emulator:  http://10.0.2.2:<port>       (alias for the host machine's loopback —
#                        works regardless of which Wi-Fi network the host is on)
#   - iOS simulator:     http://localhost:<port>       (simulator shares the host's network stack)
API_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000
